Below is a letter sent out to Janet users in the UK. It is 
the beginning of the end of the 'free' internet in the UK.

The letter suggests that news groups will be excluded if the
contents are considered offensive. Offensive to who ? Mary Whitehouse ?
 

It also says the same for
news groups that possibly contravene legislation. Does such legislation
exist in the UK ?

From:   CBS%UK.AC.UKERNA::JOHN.CAREY  2-DEC-1994 08:58:48.19
This copy of the letter sent out recently in paper form is being sent 
electronically as well and to a wider forum.

John

To: Directors at JANET-connected Institutions
cc: Members of JNUG and JANET User Groups


Dear Colleague,



As the UKERNA Manager reponsible for the UKERNA service which provides
Usenet NEWS, concerns have been drawn to my attention about the content
of certain newsgroups.

The JANET Usenet News service is provided through a contract with
EUnet GB. The service offers Usenet NEWS to all JANET-connected
institutions as an  additional benefit of JANET connection. The NEWS
provided by this service is delivered directly from EUnet GB or indirectly
through one or more intermediaries but originating from EUnet GB. 
News groups may be excluded if the contents are considered to be offensive 
or possibly contravening legislation.  News groups under and including
the following hierarchies are sent:

uk, eunet, comp, news, sci, rec, misc, soc, talk, inet, alt,
gnu,  bionet,  biz, bit, vmsnet, ddn, u3b, trial, sco, ieee,
k12, info, scot

News groups under and including alt.sex and
alt.binaries.pictures.erotica are specifically excluded.

Caveat Emptor

News is a highly valuable  service  giving  up-to-the-minute
information  on a vast range of subjects ranging from highly
serious    academic/business/technical    information     to
recreational/frivolous.   As such it has all the benefits of
openness combined with all its  dangers.  We  can  stop  the
transmission  of  obviously  suspect material, but we cannot
stop potentially offensive material escaping in  hierarchies
of  an  apparently  serious  nature. In this case the "buyer
must beware..." the  only  sure  way  to  avoid  potentially
offensive material is to not subscribe at all.

This list of groups is subject to change, of course, as newsgroups come
and go over time. EUnet GB can advise on whether a particular group is
distributed or not.

We are aware that there are institutions connected to JANET where
news feeds are taken from sources other than EUnet GB. Unless such feeds are
agreed in advance with EUnet GB (eg for the purpose of providing a backup),
their existence if received over JANET runs contrary to JANET Acceptable Use. 
This is because they have the potential both to waste scarce resources 
(particularly international bandwidth) and to act counter to the JISC's wish to
have a means of controlling the distribution of unsuitable material should
this prove necessary. News obtained via the news feeds obtained through
EUnet GB use international links belonging to EUnet GB or other
network providors and so do not make use of JANET's international
links or contibute to the traffic on them.

Therefore I urge you to arrange your news feed only via EUnet GB, and to
make it clear to individual departments in your institution that this
is the only acceptable means of receiving a news feed via JANET and
indeed that news traffic on JANET should not be generated unless such is
agreed in advance with EUnet GB.

Queries about the NEWS service should be sent to 
support@britain.eu.net.
If you wish to raise a query about NEWS with UKERNA,
please contact John Carey (J.Carey@ukerna.ac.uk) who
is the UKERNA person responsible for the contract with GBnet, or 
the undersigned.

Yours sincerely,




Phil Jones
Manager, Network Services


